WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.720
Tick bites are common if you spend time in grassy or wooded areas, and the most important

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.760
thing to remember is that early removal is the best way to reduce your risk of infection.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.200
While a bite might just look like a small red bump at first, ticks can transmit serious

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.440
illnesses like Lyme disease.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.520
When it comes to removing a tick, please ignore those old home remedies like using heat, nail

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.719
polish or petroleum jelly.

00:00:24.719 --> 00:00:29.840
Instead, use fine-tipped tweezers to grasp the tick as close to the skin's surface as

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:30.840
you can.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.720
Pull upward with steady, even pressure without twisting or jerking, which helps ensure you

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.720
remove the entire tick.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.119
Once it's out, wash the site with soap and water and apply an antiseptic.

00:00:42.119 --> 00:00:47.680
It is recommended to place the tick in a sealed bag or container labeled with the date, time

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.160
and where on the body it was found.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.360
This is incredibly helpful for doctors or public health officials if you need a medical

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.720
evaluation later, as they can identify the species or even test the tick itself to decide

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.200
on the best treatment.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.639
Finally, keep a close watch on the area for several weeks.

00:01:04.639 --> 00:01:09.480
If you notice a bull's-eye rash or if the person develops flu-like symptoms, a fever

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.120
or joint pain, they should see a health care provider right away and be sure to tell them

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.000
about the tick bite.

